<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1050–<a href="/years/1106">1106</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-dynasty">Salian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1056 and emperor from 1084. His clash with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> over the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>, and he did penance at Canossa in 1077. He also fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rebellion, an episode described by the chronicler Lampert of Hersfeld, and was eventually deposed by his son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor" width="247" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_4_g.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born on 11 November 1050, most likely at his father's palace at Goslar, the son of Emperor Henry III and Agnes of Poitou. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-dynasty">Salian dynasty</a>, which governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1024 to 1125. His father named him successor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1050, and at the age of six he became sole ruler while his mother acted as regent. In April 1062 Archbishop Anno II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and other nobles removed her in the Coup of Kaiserswerth, luring Henry onto a ship on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>; he leapt into the river and was rescued by Egbert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. Henry was girded with a sword at Worms on 29 March 1065.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around 1069 Henry set out to recover royal estates lost during his minority, most of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. He used Swabian ministeriales as officials and built royal castles garrisoned by Swabian troops, which angered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, and he put down revolts there in 1069 and 1071. His insistence on appointing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots brought conflict with Pope Alexander II, who excommunicated some of his advisers in 1073.</p><p>After crushing a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rebellion in 1075 he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and appointed Tedald archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> against the wishes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>, who threatened excommunication. At Worms on 24 January 1076 two archbishops and twenty-six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> declared Gregory's election invalid. Gregory excommunicated Henry, and in 1077 Henry sought absolution in his penitential walk to Canossa. German opponents then elected Rudolf of Rheinfelden king on 14 March 1077. Excommunicated again in 1080, Henry was supported by an antipope, Clement III, who crowned him emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1084.</p><p>From 1089 to 1096 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a> and Welf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>; his son Conrad turned against him in 1093. In 1103 he proclaimed the first imperial peace covering all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. His diplomas protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Speyer and Worms from violence and forced baptism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In December 1104 his younger son Henry deserted him, and in 1105 forced his abdication. Henry fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in February 1106, where the townspeople received him, and he sought allies to regain the throne. He fell ill and died in 1106 without absolution. His conflicts with the popes, his sons and his subjects produced a rich polemical literature in his lifetime. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Saxon War praised him as pious, while Lampert of Hersfeld said he had inherited a peaceful realm and rendered it bloodstained and a prey to internal conflicts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His daughter Adelaide of Susa ruled for over half a century, married Otto of Savoy, and was one of the most powerful figures in northern Italy, the mother-in-law of <b>Emperor Henry IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-turin">March of Turin</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1027 the house of Canossa held the march; Countess Matilda (ruled 1076–1115) hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where <b>Emperor Henry IV</b> sought Pope Gregory VII's forgiveness, and fought for the papacy for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-tuscany">March of Tuscany</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Investiture Controversy, <b>Emperor Henry IV</b> rewarded Patriarch Sigehard of Aquileia for his loyalty by granting him the county of Friuli in 1077, soon followed by Istria and Carniola.</blockquote><a href="/state/patriarchate-of-aquileia">Patriarchate of Aquileia</a></li><li><blockquote>Its scholars included the chronicler Lambert of Hersfeld, who wrote a major account of the reign of <b>Henry IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hersfeld">Principality of Hersfeld</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Imperial Palace of Goslar, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Liège, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.9,10.43&amp;year=1086" aria-label="Imperial Palace of Goslar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5547 5647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5547 5647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.9,10.43&amp;year=1086">What was here in 1086? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1016–1056</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1086–1125</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…through wealth you have obtained favor; through favor, the power of the sword; and through the power of the sword, the papal seat, which is the seat of peace; and then from the seat of peace you have expelled peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pope Gregory VII, January 1076 &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; written for the synod of Worms that declared the pope deposed &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/1075GregoryVIIlettersandconflict1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Come down, come down, and be accursed through all the ages.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pope Gregory VII, January 1076, the closing words &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; written for the synod of Worms that declared the pope deposed &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/1075GregoryVIIlettersandconflict1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
